roof has not yet examined this claim.\n\n  1. ##### Read the original work\n\nArithmetic Polyhedra ↗\n\nDaniel Allcock; Pat Devlin; Anna Felikson; Alex Kontorovich; Ian Whitehead.\n\n  2. ##### See the proposed checks\n\nSource-lock the TeX bundle; independently verify arithmeticity and commensurability invariants for the three seed polyhedra; reconstruct the gluing classification for ideal right-angled cases; then trace the reduction from the Koebe-Andreev-Thurston correspondence to the stated conjecture with specialist hyperbolic-geometry review.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDynamical Systems (https://borowiecki.dev/list/math.DS/new)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; Title: A five-species counterexample to the Hofbauer--Kon--Saito conjecture on qualitative permanence ... It persists under small perturbations, including weak additional interactions, so every sign pattern that satisfies (C1) and (C2) and contains ours is a counterexample, and it occurs for an open cone of growth vectors.\n\n[78] arXiv:2610.38374 (replaced) [pdf, html, other]\n    \n\nTitle: A five-species counterexample to the Hofbauer--Kon--Saito conjecture on qualitative permanence\n\nBeldiman Miruna-Mihaela, Halanay Andrei-Dan\n\nComments: 22 pages, 3 figures. Ancillary files: four verification scripts (Julia, standard library only)\n\nSubjects: Dynamical Systems (math.DS); Populations and Evolution (q-bio.PE)\n\nHofbauer, Kon and Saito showed that a Lotka-Volterra interaction matrix $A$ with negative diagonal can be qualitatively permanent only if (C1) every cycle of length $\\ge 2$ of $A$ is nonpositive and (C2) every negative cycle contains exactly one negative entry. They conjectured that these conditions are also sufficient, and proved this for $n\\le 3$. We give a $5\\times 5$ integer matrix with seven nonzero off-diagonal entries that satisfies (C1) and (C2) but is not qualitatively permanent. For an explicit integer growth vector, in which one species is an obligate consumer as the definition allows, the system has an interior equilibrium, and an asymptotically stable heteroclinic cycle on the boundary of the orthant attracts an open set of interior orbits. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-10-30 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-10-30)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; The 3-Color 2-Chromatic Gallai-Ramsey Number for Complete Graphs ... We study a problem posed by Kardoš asking whether the truncation of every connected simple bridgeless cubic graph has strong chromatic index six. ... In this paper, we build on the analysis initiated by Averkov and Schymura (2022) and prove that the shifted Heller constant for matrices with $m$ rows is at most $m^2 + m$ for all sufficiently large $m$. ... We show its Galois group is $S_7$, so on that conjecture no expression in radicals exists. ... Based on these data, we also propose two conjectures that are closely related to the case $Δ=3$ of Seymour's exact conjecture and to Grötzsch's conjecture on the $3$-edge-colorability of planar graphs.\n\ncomplete graph graph\n\n2026-10-30\n\nMark Budden, Elijah DeJonge\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nThe 3-Color 2-Chromatic Gallai-Ramsey Number for Complete Graphs\n\nFor $1\\le i\\le j\\le k$, let $gr^3_2(K_i, K_j, K_k)$ be the least $n\\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that every rainbow triangle-free $3$-coloring of $K_n$ (using red, blue, and green) contains a red/blue $K_i$, a red/green $K_j$, or a blue/green $K_k$. In this paper, we completely evaluate $gr^3_2(K_i, K_j, K_k)$ for all values of $i$, $j$, and $k$ in which $r(K_i,K_j)$ is known.\n\ncomplete graph contain gallai-ramsey number graph coloring\n\n2026-10-30\n\nDomenico Labbate, Federico Romaniello\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nStrong edge-colourings of truncations of cubic graphs\n\nWe study a problem posed by Kardoš asking whether the truncation of every connected simple bridgeless cubic graph has strong chromatic index six. The truncation $T(G)$ is obtained by replacing each vertex of $G$ with a triangle and attaching its incident edges to distinct vertices of that triangle. An affirmative answer was previously established for prism bases by Han and Cui. We prove that $\\sidx(T(G))=6$ whenever $G$ is a connected simple cubic graph that is bipartite or is both bridgeless and claw-free. Both proofs are constructive and use a common compatibility criterion for combining colourings of smaller pieces. The bipartite result also extends to non-det-extremal multigraphs.\n\nclaw-free cubic graph graph coloring\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) On Combinatorial Generation of Prefix Normal Words (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/259893453_On_Combinatorial_Generation_of_Prefix_Normal_Words)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.8 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; growth ratio of preﬁ x normal ... Finite prefix normal words were introduced in [12] and further studied in [5,6,9,22]. ... ...   * Jan 2026\n\nLemma 4. The number of preﬁx normal words grows exponentially in n. We have that\n\npnw(n)≥2 b n/2 c.\n\nThe ﬁrst members of the sequence pnw(n) are listed in [25], and these values suggest\n\nthat the lower bound above is not sharp. We turn our attention to the growth rate of\n\npnw(n) as n increases. Note that 1 ≤pnw(n)/pnw(n−1) ≤2. The lower bound follows\n\nform the fact that all preﬁx normal words can be extended by adding a 0 to the end, and\n\nthe upper bound is implied by the preﬁx-closed property of L PN. Fig. 6 (left) shows the\n\ngrowth ratio for small values of n. The ﬁgure shows two interesting phenomena: the values\n\nseem to approach 2 slowly, i.e., the number of preﬁx normal words almost doubles as we\n\nincrease the length by 1. Second, the 